+# Decision: Change Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard serves up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across 40 weekend markets, with 10–25 editors on a busy market and opening-time bursts reaching 20 edits per second. Editors submit commands over HTTPS, and each committed edit receives one authoritative event revision. Browsers must display committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity and recover the latest board after disconnects of up to 20 minutes.
+
+Three downstream design nodes—the change store, delivery, and browser sync—must be designed around how committed changes reach browsers. All three delivery models in this domain can meet the 15-second freshness goal, and the HLD permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure, so the choice turns on tradeoffs between latency, operational burden, and vendor coupling.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+All candidate approaches can satisfy the 15-second freshness target and preserve a single authoritative event revision; those shared capabilities are background rather than selection criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence and predictable cost** (**major**): Avoiding recurring per-connection or per-message realtime vendor fees and third-party provider coupling.
+- **Update latency** (**moderate**): Delivering committed edits to connected browsers with subsecond latency during opening-time bursts rather than multi-second polling intervals.
+- **Operational simplicity** (**moderate**): Avoiding stateful connection capacity management, WebSocket fan-out infrastructure, and custom reconnect/catch-up failure modes.
+- **Downstream surface area** (**minor**): Implementation complexity imposed across the change store, delivery, and browser sync nodes.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+- **Description**: Browsers periodically request changes over HTTPS since their last known event revision on a five-second interval. The change store serves cursor-based revision queries, delivery remains stateless request/response, and browsers run a polling loop with backoff on errors.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable cost*: **strong** — Uses only standard HTTPS endpoints with no third-party realtime provider or recurring messaging fees.
+  - *Update latency*: **weak** — Introduces up to five seconds of normal lag between commit and display, missing subsecond updates during busy opening bursts.
+  - *Operational simplicity*: **strong** — Stateless HTTP requests require no persistent socket capacity planning, session affinity, or fan-out broker.
+  - *Downstream surface area*: **strong** — Change store needs only cursor queries; delivery stays request/response; browsers need only a polling loop and connectivity-failure indicator.
+- **Summary**: Simplest stateless operation and no vendor coupling, at the cost of repeated reads and multi-second update lag.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+- **Description**: Delivery maintains persistent WebSocket connections to browsers and pushes committed edits as they are published. The change store provides replayable revision history and reliable publication, delivery manages WebSocket sessions and subscription authorization, and browsers handle reconnects and revision catch-up.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable cost*: **strong** — Runs entirely on owned infrastructure with no realtime vendor bill or provider lock-in.
+  - *Update latency*: **strong** — Pushes committed revisions immediately to connected browsers, delivering subsecond updates under normal connectivity.
+  - *Operational simplicity*: **weak** — Requires owning WebSocket connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, session lifecycle management, and reconnect storms after transient drops.
+  - *Downstream surface area*: **partial** — Requires reliable publication and replayable revisions in the change store, session state in delivery, and reconnect/catch-up logic in browser sync.
+- **Summary**: Delivers fast updates without a realtime vendor bill, while taking on connection capacity and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+- **Description**: The server publishes committed revisions to a managed third-party realtime provider, and browsers subscribe through that provider's client SDK and channel credentials. The change store reconciles publication state, delivery wraps provider authentication and publishing, and browsers use the provider's subscription model.
+- **Analysis**:
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable cost*: **weak** — Introduces recurring vendor fees and couples delivery and browser sync to a specific provider's SDK, auth model, and reliability characteristics.
+  - *Update latency*: **strong** — Delivers subsecond updates over managed persistent connections.
+  - *Operational simplicity*: **partial** — Offloads persistent connection scaling and edge fan-out to the provider, though publication reconciliation and credential minting remain in-house.
+  - *Downstream surface area*: **partial** — Change store needs publication reconciliation, delivery wraps provider APIs, and browser sync integrates the provider SDK alongside revision catch-up.
+- **Summary**: Achieves low-latency updates with less connection-scaling work, in exchange for recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-Hosted WebSockets** is selected.
+
+It is the only option that scores **strong** on both **vendor independence and predictable cost** (major) and **update latency** (moderate). Five-second HTTPS polling avoids stateful connections but gives up subsecond updates and generates repeated read traffic; a hosted realtime service avoids connection-scaling operations but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. Choosing self-hosted WebSockets accepts the operational responsibility for WebSocket capacity and reconnect handling as an explicit tradeoff.
+
+This choice aligns with the HLD tenet permitting self-hosted infrastructure and obligates the downstream nodes as follows:
+- **Change store**: Must persist replayable event revisions (supporting the 20-minute disconnect recovery goal and 7-day retention) and reliably publish committed edits to delivery.
+- **Delivery**: Must manage WebSocket sessions, enforce server-side subscription authorization (per the server-side authorization tenet), and handle fan-out for 800 simultaneous browsers at 20 edits/sec bursts.
+- **Browser sync**: Must maintain WebSocket subscriptions, perform reconnect and revision catch-up, and surface connectivity failures visibly rather than appearing unchanged (per the connectivity-visibility tenet).
